---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import EvidenceLedger from '../../components/EvidenceLedger.astro';
import { getPath } from '../../utils/paths';
import { loadVerificationData } from '../../utils/verification-loader';
import '../../styles/kernels.css';

const data = loadVerificationData();
const { stats } = data;
const formatCount = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');

// The explorer reads these specs from the page. A ledger's `page_url` is
// site-relative in the data, and the deployed site may sit under a base path,
// so it is resolved here, once, before it is embedded.
const specs = data.explorerSpecs.map((spec) =>
  spec.ledger ? { ...spec, ledger: { ...spec.ledger, page_url: getPath(spec.ledger.page_url) } } : spec,
);
const kernelAreas = [...new Set(specs.map((s) => s.area))];

// The catalog renderer escapes everything it does not emit itself, so the
// embedded HTML cannot hold a closing script tag; `<` is escaped anyway so no
// future field can end the JSON block early.
const specsJson = JSON.stringify(specs).replace(/</g, '\\u003c');

const facts: { value: string; label: string }[] = [
  stats.catalogAvailable
    ? { value: `${formatCount(stats.explorerKernels)} of ${formatCount(stats.functions)}`, label: 'proved kernel functions run here' }
    : { value: formatCount(stats.explorerKernels), label: 'proved kernel functions run here' },
  { value: formatCount(stats.models), label: 'Stateright models' },
  { value: formatCount(stats.pinnedTotal), label: 'pinned unique states' },
];
if (stats.catalogAvailable) {
  facts.push({ value: formatCount(stats.proofSessions), label: 'proof sessions in the ledger' });
}
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Verification Playground"
  description="Evaluate Krabka's Creusot-verified decision kernels in WebAssembly, read each kernel's proof ledger row, and browse every Stateright model with its bounds, properties, and pinned state counts."
  currentSlug="/docs/verification-playground"
>
  <div class="space-y-6 font-sans">
    <div class="border-b border-white/10 pb-4">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-extrabold text-white sm:text-4xl">Verification Playground</h1>
      <p class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs text-gray-400" aria-label="Verification figures">
        {facts.map((fact, i) => (
          <>
            {i > 0 && <span class="text-gray-600 select-none" aria-hidden="true">·</span>}
            <span><span class="font-semibold text-white tabular-nums">{fact.value}</span> {fact.label}</span>
          </>
        ))}
      </p>
      <p class="text-gray-400 mt-2 text-sm max-w-2xl leading-relaxed">
        Run the proved kernels in your browser, and read what each Stateright model checks.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Krabka keeps its safety-critical decisions in <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">krabka-verified</code>,
        a crate of small pure functions whose contracts Creusot proves. The kernels do no I/O, so the crate
        compiles to WebAssembly unchanged, and the explorer below calls the same compiled bodies the broker
        calls: the answer on screen is the broker's answer, shown beside
        the <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">requires</code> and <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">ensures</code> contract
        quoted from the source.
      </p>
      <p>
        The kernels here are {formatCount(stats.explorerKernels)} of the crate's decision functions, chosen
        because their inputs fit a form.
        The <a href={getPath('/docs/broker/verification')} class="text-krab-400 hover:text-krab-300 font-semibold">verification catalog</a> lists
        every proved kernel and every model, and each kernel's panel below quotes the catalog's row for it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Kernel explorer -->
    <h2 id="kernels" class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Creusot-verified kernel explorer</h2>
    <p class="text-sm text-gray-400 -mt-2">
      Areas: {kernelAreas.join(', ')}. Pick a kernel, edit its inputs, and the result updates as you type. An
      input that breaks a precondition is reported rather than evaluated, because the proof says nothing about
      inputs outside the contract.{' '}
      {stats.catalogAvailable
        ? 'Each kernel’s panel has a “Proof ledger” drilldown quoting the catalog: what the kernel proves, who calls it in production, which proof sessions cover it, and what the caller must establish.'
        : 'The catalog rows are missing from this build, so the “Proof ledger” drilldown under each kernel is absent; run the docs sync to restore them.'}
    </p>

    <script type="application/json" id="krabka-kernel-specs" is:inline set:html={specsJson}></script>
    <div id="krabka-kernels" class="not-prose">
      <p class="kx-fallback">
        Loading the verified kernel explorer. This panel needs JavaScript and WebAssembly. If it does not
        appear, your browser may have them disabled.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Stateright models -->
    <h2 id="models" class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-4">Stateright models</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        Where correctness depends on a sequence of crashes, retries and interleavings rather than on one
        decision, Krabka writes a Stateright model. The checker enumerates every reachable state within the
        model's bounds and prints a counterexample trace if a property fails, and each runner asserts its
        unique-state count against a pinned constant, so a search that silently shrinks fails the build.
      </p>
      <p>
        The models cannot run in this page: they live as test modules inside broker and client crates that
        depend on networking and file I/O, and the checker itself uses threads. The list below gives the
        inventory instead. Its {formatCount(stats.models)} entry points are grouped by area, and each opens to
        the production code it drives, its bounds, its properties, its pinned unique states
        ({formatCount(stats.pinnedTotal)} between them), what stays outside the model, and one level further
        down the catalog's own paragraphs about it. Every figure comes from the verification catalog, which
        pull request CI keeps in step with the models.
      </p>
    </div>

    <EvidenceLedger
      models={data.models}
      modelNotes={data.catalog.modelNotes}
      initialTab="models"
      id="model-ledger"
      catalogPath={getPath('/docs/broker/verification')}
    />

    <details class="group rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.02] not-prose">
      <summary class="flex cursor-pointer select-none list-none items-center justify-between gap-3 px-4 py-3">
        <h2 id="how-it-works" class="text-base font-bold text-white">How the explorer works</h2>
        <svg class="h-3.5 w-3.5 shrink-0 text-gray-500 transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-90" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" aria-hidden="true">
          <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 5l7 7-7 7" />
        </svg>
      </summary>
      <div class="space-y-3 border-t border-white/10 px-4 py-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-gray-300">
        <ol class="list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5">
          <li>The site build compiles <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">krabka-verified</code> at the pinned broker release into the same WebAssembly module as the consensus playground.</li>
          <li>The page serialises your inputs to JSON and hands them to one dispatcher function in the module.</li>
          <li>The dispatcher checks the kernel's <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">requires</code> clauses first. Inputs outside the contract come back as a precondition report and never reach the kernel.</li>
          <li>Inputs inside the contract run the proved body, and the result comes back as JSON for the page to render.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          The bindings live in <code class="font-mono text-krab-300">playground/src/kernels.rs</code> in this
          repository. They add no logic of their own: each arm parses a request, checks the preconditions the
          catalog lists for the caller, and calls the kernel. The proof ledger text under each kernel is read
          from the catalog at build time, so the page and the catalog cannot disagree.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>

    <div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-white pt-2">Related Resources</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose pt-1">
        <a href={getPath('/docs/playground')} class="p-4 rounded-xl frosted-card border border-white/10 hover:border-krab-500/50 transition-colors block">
          <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Consensus Playground →</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1">Drive the deterministic KRaft engine itself: partitions, dropped and reordered messages, elections.</p>
        </a>
        <a href={getPath('/verification')} class="p-4 rounded-xl frosted-card border border-white/10 hover:border-krab-500/50 transition-colors block">
          <div class="text-sm font-bold text-white">Correctness & Verification →</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1">Safe Rust, Creusot proofs, Stateright models, and where the formal boundary ends.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

</DocsLayout>

<script src={getPath('/playground/kernels.js')} type="module" is:inline></script>
